 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{iters} \cdot N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples} \cdot C_{MLP})$, where $N_{iters}$ is the number of iterations, $N_{rand}$ is the batch size (rays per step), $N_{samples}$ is the number of samples per ray, and $C_{MLP}$ is the cost of a single MLP query [S5, S14, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{rand} \cdot N_{samples} \cdot C_{MLP})$ for the forward pass, plus $O(N_{iters})$ if storing intermediate states for reverse-mode AD [44-radiance-field-optimization.md]. Chunking reduces peak memory usage to $O(\frac{N_{rand}}{ChunkSize} \cdot N_{samples} \cdot C_{MLP})$ [S5, S7, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Training on 128x128 images typically takes 250 epochs [S4, unofficial]. For a dataset of 199 images down-sampled by 4, training with a batch size of 1024 results in ~7,800 iterations per epoch [S17, unofficial].
-*   **Scaling:** Performance scales with the number of training images and resolution. Larger batch sizes (e.g., $2^{14}$) can improve throughput but may hinder data loading performance and reduce GPU occupancy, while smaller batch sizes (e.g., $2^{10}$) cap iterations per second [S15, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** End-to-end differentiable, handles complex geometry and appearance, produces high-quality novel views.
-*   **Limitations:** Slow convergence due to the need for many iterations (100k+), high memory usage for large scenes, prone to artifacts (floaters) in unobserved regions [S1][S17, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** When high-quality novel view synthesis is required and sufficient multi-view images are available.
-*   **When Not to Use:** When real-time rendering is required, or when only sparse views are available without additional regularization/initialization [S1, 9394-sparse-view-and-initialization-methods.md].
+*   **Time Complexity:** $O(N_{iters} \cdot N_{rand} \cdot K)$, where $N_{iters}$ is the number of iterations, $N_{rand}$ is the batch size (rays per step), and $K$ is the number of samples per ray [S5, S14, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_{rand} \cdot K)$ for storing intermediate rendering states during the forward pass, plus $O(\Theta)$ for model weights [S5, 44-radiance-field-optimization.md, unofficial].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Training typically requires 100,000 to 1,000,000 iterations [S11, S14, unofficial]. For a scene with 199 images, training for 5 epochs with batch size 1024 results in ~39,000 iterations [S17, unofficial].
+*   **Scaling:** Performance scales with batch size, but larger batches hinder data loading performance and prevent full GPU occupancy, while smaller batches cap iterations per second [S15, unofficial]. A batch size of $2^{10}$ is often chosen as a tradeoff [S15, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, differentiable end-to-end, produces high-quality novel views [S5, S14, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Slow convergence due to non-convex optimization, high memory usage for large scenes, prone to artifacts like floaters in sparse views [S1][S17, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** Standard novel view synthesis with dense input views [S5, unofficial].
+*   **When Not to Use:** Real-time rendering (requires acceleration methods like [Acceleration and Sparse Structures](11405-acceleration-and-sparse-structures.md)), sparse-view settings without regularization ([Sparse View and Initialization Methods](9394-sparse-view-and-initialization-methods.md)), or when camera poses are unknown ([Camera Pose and Parameter Optimization](7611-camera-pose-and-parameter-optimization.md)).
 
 ## Background
-The NeRF Training Loop was introduced in the seminal paper "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S5, S18, unofficial]. It builds on the concept of coordinate-based neural networks for implicit scene representation [S3, unofficial][S20]. The method leverages the differentiability of volume rendering to optimize the neural network parameters via gradient descent, minimizing the photometric error between rendered and ground-truth images [44-radiance-field-optimization.md]. Since its introduction, it has become a foundational technique in 3D computer vision, inspiring numerous variants and applications in novel view synthesis, 3D reconstruction, and text-to-3D generation [S27].
+The NeRF Training Loop was introduced by Mildenhall et al. in "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" in 2020 [S5, S18, unofficial]. It builds on the concept of coordinate-based neural networks for implicit representations [S3, unofficial]. The method was initially implemented in TensorFlow [S5, unofficial] and later ported to PyTorch [S6, S11, unofficial]. It has become the foundational training procedure for numerous variants, including HashNeRF [S7, unofficial], JaxNeRF [S25], and DreamFusion [S27].
 
